1 NOTE Mattias, after thorough military training in Sweden, went first to Poland in 1699 and then, in 1700 to Riga, Latvia, which then was Swedish territory. After a few years in Swedish miltitary service in Livonia, Mattias was sent to 
2 CONC Stockholm with a group of Russian war prisoners. He continued his Swedish military service until 1719. He moved to Vesterås, but in 1723 returned to Livonia. Two years earlier, at the peace settlement in Nystad, Sweden had given u
2 CONC p her Baltic provinces and earlier inhabitants could get back confiscated properties by swearing loyalty to the Tsar. Mattias had good reason to swear such loyalty, because in 1705 he had married Christina Elisabeth d'Albedyll, wh
2 CONC o belonged to a Livonian noble family, and its likely that either through his wife, or in his own behalf, he owned property in Livonia. Only four of their six children are known.
2 CONT
2 CONT He joined the Russian army and became a Captain, stationed in Pernau (Pärnu) in Estonia.
1 NOTE Mattias, after thorough military training in Sweden, went first to Poland in 1699 and then, in 1700 to Riga, Latvia, which then was Swedish territory. After a few years in Swedish miltitary service in Livonia, Mattias was sent to 
2 CONC Stockholm with a group of Russian war prisoners. He continued his Swedish military service until 1719. He moved to Vesterås, but in 1723 returned to Livonia. Two years earlier, at the peace settlement in Nystad, Sweden had given u
2 CONC p her Baltic provinces and earlier inhabitants could get back confiscated properties by swearing loyalty to the Tsar. Mattias had good reason to swear such loyalty, because in 1705 he had married Christina Elisabeth d'Albedyll, wh
2 CONC o belonged to a Livonian noble family, and its likely that either through his wife, or in his own behalf, he owned property in Livonia. Only four of their six children are known.
2 CONT
2 CONT He joined the Russian army and became a Captain, stationed in Pernau (Pärnu) in Estonia.
