1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/72518082/Johannes-Van%20Hoesen
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 11--Will of John Vanhosen, of Anson Co., son John, Dau. Yonkey, son Valentine, dau. Catherine, dau. Christian, dau. Mary, son Jacob, 150 acres, son Abram 150 acres, dau. Elizabeth tract I bou.  of John Davis. Jany 21, 1762.
2 CONT Edmund Lilly                           John X Vanhosen John X Watson
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes grew up in Claverack.  When he was 13, a group of Palatine Germans settled in the area ourth of Claverack at Tar Bush and East Camp.  Among them was a young, orphaned girl, Elizabeth Christina Laux, whom Johnnes met and f
2 CONC ell in love with.  In 1720 they married and settled for the next 8 years in the area.  As it got crowded, he moved with some Germans to Tulpehocken, PN.  His brother-in-law, Abraham Laux or Loucks, had moved to the area 3 years be
2 CONC fore.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes took up land on what was called the William Allen Tract in Heidelberg Township.  Here he remained until his family was all born and most of them had grown to maturity.  When his second son, Valentine, moved to North Carol
2 CONC ina, he decide to move, too.  On the 5th of April, 1753, he sold his land to John Joseph Derr and Henry Boyer, and at the age of 56,moved to North Carolina and settled in Anson Co.  See source for copy of deed. Source Link: https:
2 CONC //www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000175883602828label=@S300@ Source Link: https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000175882890825label=@S22@
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT NAME: Johannes Van Hosen (John 2, Jan Fransse 1), was the first man to take on the surname of Van Hooser. The change in the surname, no doubt, came through an English interpretation of the name, which changed the spelling first to
2 CONC  Van Hoosen and then, since the "n" looked like an "r" when written, it was altered to the name it is written today; SOURCE: Van Hooser in America, by Joyce Lindstrom, page 7; RESEARCH: Sherry Smith.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIRTH: Johannes Van Hoesen was born in Claverack, Albany (now Columbia), New York on land that was purchased from his grandfather, Jan Fransse Van Husen in 1662. He was the son of Johannes Van Hosen and his first wife, Jannitje (J
2 CONC ane) Janse de Ryck. He was christened 1 Aug 1697 at the Dutch Reformed Church at Kingston, Ulster, New York, which is across the Hudson River and down the river a ways. In those days, the people went where the traveling minister w
2 CONC as, who happened to be Justus Falckner. The book, The World of Justus Falckner by Delbert Wallace Clark tells about the minister's travels in the early days of New York's settlements; SOURCE: Van Hooser in America, by Joyce Lindst
2 CONC rom, page 7; RESEARCH: Sherry Smith.
2 CONT
2 CONT BOUNDARIES: Claverack, Albany (now Columbia) New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT MARRIAGE: East Camp, Albany, New York; SOURCE: Van Hooser Book by Joyce Lindstrom, page 15.∼Name listed as Johannes Hannessen Van Husum on the U.S., Dutch Reformed Church Records in Selected States, 1639-1989 (Marriage) ~ ancestry
2 CONC .com
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Claverack, Albany County (now Columbia County, New York Colony, British America.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/55843028/johannes-hannessen-van_hooser Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projec
2 CONC ts/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Feb 14 2022, 20:33:44 UTC''
